   Question 1 - Reserved Characters

    <p>
        In HTML, use &lt;html&gt; to define the HTML document.
    </p>

    <p>
        The condition x &gt; 10 means that x is greater than 10.
    </p>

    <p>
        HTML uses &amp; to represent the ampersand character.
    </p>


    <h3>Hexadecimal Entities</h3>

    <p>
        The less-than symbol is written as &#x3C; in hexadecimal.
    </p>

    <p>
        The greater-than symbol is written as &#x3E; in hexadecimal.
    </p>

    <p>
        The ampersand symbol is written as &#x26; in hexadecimal.
    </p>




    Question 2 - Currency Symbols

    <p>
        Euro using named entity: &euro;50
    </p>

    <p>
        Euro using hexadecimal entity: &#x20AC;50
    </p>

    <p>
        Pound using named entity: &pound;75
    </p>

    <p>
        Pound using decimal entity: &#163;75
    </p>

    <p>
        Yen using named entity: &yen;5000
    </p>



 
    Question 3 - Mathematical Symbols

    <p>
        The statement 10 &ne; 5 means that 10 is not equal to 5.
    </p>

    <p>
        The summation formula is &#x2211; x = 100.
    </p>

    <p>
        A mathematical value can continue to infinity: x = &#8734;.
    </p>



   
